User Input

While the name asked the user for the input, the price used float, which accepts decimal numbers, allowing the string to register it as a number. Later the price is multiplied to the quantity, without the float before the input, there would be an error.
2.
print("Enter the following information about an item you wish to purchase..")




name=input(("The name of the item:"))


price = float(input("The price: $"))


quantity= int(input(("How many do you want?")))


subtotal = price * quantity
tax = subtotal * 0.13
total = subtotal + tax


print()
print(f"You choose to buy {quantity} {name}.")
print(f"That will come out to ${total}")

3. The prompt is what is put in the input() to give directions in the output. This code is essentially setting the variable “name” to the input, but it is not printing it out. Rather, it is printing out "Enter the name of the item:"

4. Int is used to collect a whole number from the user and convert it so that it is usable for the computer. Float is the same as int except it allows for decimals as well. If you remove them and then have the computer do an equation with them, they will not count as numbers but rather strings, causing an error. 


